{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was involved in the politics of the Crusader states from an early age. He was a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the ongoing conflicts with Muslim forces.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n\n**Early Life and Reign**\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140. He was raised in Tripoli and educated in the ways of governance and warfare. His early life was marked by the influence of his mother Hodierna who managed the county during his minority. Raymond III was knighted in 1156 and began taking on more responsibilities in the county. He became fully independent ruler in 1160 after his mother stepped aside. His early reign was marked by efforts to secure Tripoli against attacks from Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler. He also strengthened alliances with other Crusader states and European powers.\n\n**Conflict and Alliances**\n\nRaymond III was a central figure in the politics of the Crusader states. He was involved in the conflicts between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and Muslim forces led by Saladin. Raymond III sought to maintain the balance of power and often clashed with other Crusader leaders including Amalric I of Jerusalem and Guy of Lusignan. He was known for his diplomatic skills and ability to navigate complex political landscapes. Raymond III also maintained close ties with the Byzantine Empire and sought their support against Muslim forces.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n\n**Third Crusade and Death**\n\nRaymond III played a significant role in the Third Crusade. He was a key advisor to Guy of Lusignan and later to Conrad of Montferrat. His strategic insights were crucial in the defense of the Crusader states against Saladin. Raymond III died in 1187 shortly after the Battle of Hattin. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Crusader states. He was succeeded by his distant cousin Raymond IV of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n\n**Legacy**\n\nRaymond III is remembered as a capable and diplomatic ruler. His leadership during a turbulent period in Crusader history was marked by efforts to maintain stability and security. He is often overshadowed by more prominent figures like Saladin and Richard the Lionheart but his contributions were significant. Raymond III's reign saw the County of Tripoli through challenging times and his legacy endured in the region's history.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.22343038022518158}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.3783313035964966}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.856858039507642e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.013798443600535393}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.827859811484814e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.4372080862522125}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06708855926990509}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -6.079655122448457e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0004976941272616386}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.26212596893310547}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.21302545070648193}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -4.6491513785440475e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009941878961399198}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00048029806930571795}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.0490362910786644e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00013386306818574667}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0012444854946807027}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0001754606782924384}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.7576668858528137}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.008646